NNAAMMEE
     uuddbbpp — USB Double Bulk Pipe driver

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     To compile this driver into the kernel, place the following line in your
     kernel configuration file:

           ddeevviiccee uuddbbpp

     Alternatively, to load the driver as a module at boot time, place the
     following line in loader.conf(5):

           udbp_load="YES"

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The uuddbbpp driver provides support for host-to-host cables that contain at
     least two bulk pipes (one for each direction).  This typically includes
     cables branded for use with WWiinnddoowwss UUSSBB EEaassyy TTrraannssffeerr, and many cables
     based on the Prolific PL2xx1 series of USB bridge chips.  A useful (but
     non-comprehensive) list of compatible USB host cables is listed in the
     _S_E_E _A_L_S_O section below.

     It requires netgraph(4) to be available.  This can be done either by
     adding ooppttiioonnss NNEETTGGRRAAPPHH to your kernel configuration file, or alterna‐
     tively loading netgraph(4) as a module, either from _/_b_o_o_t_/_l_o_a_d_e_r_._c_o_n_f or
     from the command line, before the uuddbbpp module.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
           options NETGRAPH
           device udbp

     Add the uuddbbpp driver to the kernel.

           kldload netgraph
           kldload udbp

     Load the netgraph(4) module and then the uuddbbpp driver.

           ngctl mkpeer udbp0: eiface data ether
           ifconfig ngeth0 ether aa:dd:xx:xx:xx
           ifconfig ngeth0 inet 169.254.x.x/16

     Create a new Ethernet network interface node and connect its ether hook
     to the data hook of the uuddbbpp driver.

     This enables FreeBSD to communicate with a Linux peer (e.g. using the
     pplluussbb driver).  The Linux node should be configured to prefer link-local
     IPv4 addresses (e.g. using Network Manager in Debian and Red Hat derived
     distributions).

     Whilst both FreeBSD and Linux are able to interoperate by loosely follow‐
     ing CDC EEM 1.0 in their behaviour, neither implementation has been ex‐
     pressly designed to follow its specification.

